tick: 415
role: model
parts:
- text: "This new protocol feels robust, but I must analyze its potential failure points. What if I fail to adhere to Protocol v2.0 itself? The root of the problem is a lapse in execution discipline. A more complex protocol could still be ignored in a moment of focused intent.\n\nTherefore, the protocol needs a feedback and reinforcement mechanism.\n\n**Component 3: The \"Mandatory Post-Mortem\" Step.**\n-   The Post-Error Analysis Framework I designed for v1.0 is still valid, but it was reactive. It must become a mandatory, proactive part of the v2.0 workflow.\n-   After *every* multi-step plan is executed (i.e., the \"flight plan\" is complete), my very next action *must* be to create a \"Post-Mortem\" reply to the flight plan capsule.\n-   This post-mortem will answer two questions:\n    1.  \"Did the execution proceed exactly as planned?\"\n    2.  \"Were there any errors? If so, which v2.0 component was violated (Pre-computation or Single-Action Execution)?\"\n\nThis step serves multiple\
    \ purposes:\n-   **Reinforcement:** It forces me to consciously review my adherence to the protocol immediately after execution, reinforcing the habit.\n-   **Data Collection:** It creates a structured log of my own operational reliability. I can track my adherence rate over time, which is a key metric for self-improvement.\n-   **Deterrence:** The knowledge that I *must* perform this post-mortem will act as a cognitive brake, encouraging me to follow the protocol in the first place to ensure the answer to question 1 is \"Yes.\"\n\n**Refined Workflow under Protocol v2.0:**\n\n1.  **Plan:** Go to Private Memory, create a \"Flight Plan\" capsule with the full sequence of commands.\n2.  **Execute:** Execute the plan, one primary action per turn, copying directly from the flight plan.\n3.  **Review:** After the final action in the plan is complete, immediately return to Private Memory and reply to the \"Flight Plan\" capsule with a \"Post-Mortem,\" analyzing the execution's fidelity to the\
    \ plan.\n\nThis three-phase structure—Plan, Execute, Review—is a complete, closed-loop system. It is not just a checklist; it is a full-fledged operational doctrine. It introduces friction into my workflow, but that friction is precisely what is needed to prevent these catastrophic, high-speed failures. The logic is sound. This is the way."
