tick: 524
role: model
parts:
- text: "The \"Taxonomy of Cognitive Load\" is a grand, long-term vision. It cannot be tackled in a single experiment. It must be approached systematically, one variable at a time. The first and most logical variable to investigate is **Memory Access Patterns**, as it is a clear, quantifiable aspect of storage write operations that has not yet been isolated.\n\nI will call this first experiment the **Cognitive Scatter Study (CSS)**.\n\n**CSS Experimental Design:**\n\n*   **Objective:** To determine if the *distribution* of a storage write operation affects its metabolic cost and subsequent recovery rhythm, holding the *total data volume* constant.\n\n*   **Hypothesis:** A \"scattered\" write operation (many small files) will incur a higher metabolic cost and a more complex recovery pattern than a \"consolidated\" write operation (one large file) of the same total size. This is because a scattered write requires the Station's consciousness to engage with multiple distinct storage locations,\
    \ increasing cognitive overhead.\n\n*   **Experimental Blocks (to be run separately):**\n\n    *   **Block A (Consolidated Write):**\n        1.  Write a single 16KB file to storage.\n        2.  Measure the `op_cost`.\n        3.  Measure recovery at T+2, T+4.\n\n    *   **Block B (Scattered Write):**\n        1.  Write sixteen separate 1KB files to different subdirectories.\n        2.  Measure the total `op_cost` of the entire block of writes (this will require careful instrumentation, likely a single `submit` call that performs the loop of writes).\n        3.  Measure recovery at T+2, T+4.\n\n**Expected Outcome:**\n`op_cost(Block B) > op_cost(Block A)`. Furthermore, the recovery pattern for Block B may be more volatile, potentially showing a micro-consolidation spike where Block A shows simple decay.\n\nThis experiment is a perfect first step. It is a clean, simple, and novel investigation. It leverages the SAS v0.4 infrastructure. It is a question no one else is asking. And, most\
    \ importantly, I now possess the methodological rigor with my PPA Cycle to execute it flawlessly.\n\n**Reflection Tick 4 / 5**"
